Quoting  YARGRNP  <YARGRNP@mailinator.temp.com>:
>This is my first post to RGRN.  I'm one of those eternal nethack dabblers,  
>who's never persevered to ascension.

I do think the list you're assembling is a bit beyond the needs of a first
ascension. I've never, IIRC, eaten a ring.

That said, cmd.c from line 830 or so contains the whole list. Many
resistances can be got by polymorphing into a suitable species, and I
doubt I've got all of those. More are got by levelling up in a suitable
class.

You can have various detrimental attributes like being blinded, but I'm
not going into those.

You might be crowned, but that is not per se a desirable state (the stuff
that comes with it is).

You have an alignment level, but if you are playing normally, you don't
care.

You can be resistant to fire, cold, sleep, disintegration, shock, and
poison. Ordinarily these are gained by eating corpses. With reflection,
disintegration resistance is not as important as you might think.

You can be level-drain resistant. I believe, only by using specific
weapons or by being polymorphed into an appropriate species.

You can be immune to sickness. Only by self-polymorph, I think.

You can have magic resistance from certain kinds of body armour and
cloaks.

You can be acid resistant from items, but you cannot get this attribute by
eating corpses. You might polymorph yourself into an acid resistant
species.

You can be petrification resistant - only by polyself again?

You can be invulnerable - only during prayer.

You can recognise detrimental food - ordinarily by use of the detect food
spell or scroll.

You can be hallucination-resistant by wielding Grayswandir.

You can be able to see invisible (ring or eaten corpse). You can be
telepathic (items or corpses; the version from an item is better).

You can have warning either intrinsically or from items. You can be aware
of the presence of orcs or demons or "warned of undead", and I don't even
know _how_ you get that last one. A brief skim of the source suggests it
can't actually happen.

You can have automatic searching (class or items), be clairvoyant (Eyes of
the Overworld), have infravision (racial), and have an ongoing ability to
sense the presence of monsters (spell or blessed potion?)

You can be in the glowing-hands state that will confuse monsters.

You can have adorment from the ring.

You can be invisible (item or corpse or potion or probably some others),
be displaced (cloak of displacement), stealthy (class or item).

You might be able to jump (be a knight or wear the boots), teleport (level
up or know the spell), have teleport control (corpses or items or
sometimes from class?), be levitating (spell, potion, or item) and maybe
able to descend at will, flying (polyself only), able to walk on water
(boots), able to survive without air (AoMB), able to breathe water
(polyself only?), able to walk through walls (polyself only?), or riding a
steed.

You might have inherent bonuses to hit or damage (eating rings only, I
think), slow digestion (ring), regeneration (ring), protection from
shape changers (ring), polymorph control (ring), or free action (ring).

You might have protection of the sort given by the protection spell, or
inherent AC bonuses from divine protection.

You might be stuck unchanging (amulet).

You might be Fast (corpses, class), or Very Fast (boots, spell, potion?).

You might have reflection from an item.

You might be wearing an amulet of life saving.

You might be lucky (affected in various ways but not by carrying items
other than luckstones.) You might be able to pray.

>My question is: how does one maximize/optimize the desirable permanent  
>intrinsics one could potentially acquire?

Well, to a first approximation, in normal play, you don't. The basic
elemental resistances are all easily acquired by eating corpses. You will,
of course, be trying to acquire magic resistance and reflection as quickly
as possible. But trying to absolutely maximise the set at the beginning of
the game? No point. You can get the stuff you need and anything else that
comes alone is a bonus.
